    "Layout": {}
  },
  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ESGLT2 inhibitors, including dapagliflozin and empagliflozin, are used to treat heart failure in people with or without diabetes. Pharmacists can help patients understand why the medicine has been prescribed, how to use it safely and which symptoms need prompt review.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EExplaining the Medicine\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESome patients may assume they have been prescribed a diabetes medicine by mistake. Explain that this medicine is also used to help reduce the risk of heart failure hospitalisation and cardiovascular death. It causes the kidneys to remove more glucose and sodium through the urine, which also increases fluid loss.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EChecks Before Supply\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIndication:\u003C/strong\u003E Confirm that the medicine is being used for heart failure and check the current product information for the prescribed dose.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKidney function:\u003C/strong\u003E Check the latest eGFR against the product information and local guidance.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFluid status:\u003C/strong\u003E Ask about dizziness, low blood pressure, reduced fluid intake and recent diuretic changes.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDiabetes treatment:\u003C/strong\u003E Check for insulin or a sulfonylurea, as the prescriber may need to review the dose to reduce the risk of hypoglycaemia.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKetoacidosis risk:\u003C/strong\u003E Ask about type 1 diabetes, previous ketoacidosis, very low carbohydrate diets, heavy alcohol use and upcoming surgery.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInfection history:\u003C/strong\u003E Ask about recurrent genital fungal infections or urinary tract infections.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EKey Counselling Points\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ETake the medicine as directed, usually once daily, with or without food.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EKeep taking it even if symptoms improve, unless advised to stop.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPassing more urine can occur, particularly when starting treatment.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EFollow the fluid advice provided by the treating team, and report dizziness or light-headedness.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EKeep the genital area clean and dry, and report itching, discharge, unusual redness or pain.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAttend any recommended blood tests, as a small early change in eGFR can occur.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ESick Day Advice\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPatients should contact their doctor, pharmacist or heart failure team promptly if they have vomiting, diarrhoea, poor fluid intake or are unable to eat normally. The medicine may need to be paused temporarily to reduce the risk of dehydration and ketoacidosis. Patients should be told when and how to restart it, as a temporary pause can accidentally become a permanent stop.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ESurgery and Fasting\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPatients should tell their healthcare team that they take an SGLT2 inhibitor before surgery, colonoscopy or any other procedure that requires fasting. The medicine is commonly withheld for at least 3 days before major surgery or a prolonged fast, but local perioperative guidance should be followed. Restart it only when advised, usually once the person is eating and drinking normally.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhen to Seek Urgent Help\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAdvise patients to seek urgent medical care for:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ENausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, unusual tiredness or fast or difficult breathing, which may indicate ketoacidosis, even if blood glucose is normal\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESevere dizziness, fainting or signs of significant dehydration\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPain, swelling, redness or tenderness in the genital or perineal area, especially with fever or feeling unwell\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EFever, back pain or burning when passing urine, which may indicate a serious urinary tract infection\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EFollow-Up and Communication\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAt follow-up, ask about adherence, cost, side effects, sick day planning and changes to diuretics or diabetes medicines. Share any concerns with the prescriber or heart failure team, and make sure the medication list is updated whenever treatment is paused, restarted or stopped.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
